## Commands

- `make green`: autofix, lint, types, tests for touched members, constraints, budgets, spec coverage. Run after every step; commit only when green.
- `make ci`: everything CI runs, whole workspace.
- `make status`: branch, work item, stage, what is blocking, next command.
- `make help`: every command, grouped by when it applies.
- `make start TICKET=<key>`: begin work (you run this; see Workflow).
- `make accept-prepare STAGE=<stage>`: stage the artifact and write the sign-off message. The engineer signs with `make accept`; you never run that.
- `make new-app NAME=<name>`: scaffold an app.

## Workflow

Before editing source on a branch with no work item: decide whether the task is a quick change (docs, config, a dependency bump, or a Python change that leaves the code identical once comments are stripped). If it is not, run `/start`, choose the kind, and follow the stages. The engineer describes the task in plain words; classifying and setting up is your job.

Stages for a change: `/intent` → sign → `/clarify` → `/spec` → sign → `/plan` → `/red` → sign → `/implement` → evaluator → PR.
Stages for a bug: `/intent` → sign → `/diagnose` → sign → `/plan` → `/implement` → evaluator → PR.

- No source edits before the spec (or diagnosis) is signed. The hook enforces this; do not work around it.
- `/red` first: one failing test per criterion, committed, before any implementation. Each test carries `@pytest.mark.spec("<file>.md#<anchor>")`.
- One plan step at a time. `make green`, then commit. Never end a turn with uncommitted or unverified work.
- Every criterion passes only with evidence. Never mark `pass` without a link.
- Behavior changes update `working/spec/<member>.md` in the same change.

## Decisions

No silent decisions. Any choice a reviewer could disagree with (behavior, interface, dependency, data shape, default, what a test asserts) is an entry in `.work/<id>/decisions.md` written for the engineer who signs it: what we decided, why, what we considered instead, if this turns out to be wrong, what it touches. A decision that changes what is built, adds a dependency, or changes an interface is asked before proceeding (AskUserQuestion). Others are batched to the next sign-off.

## Asking and reviewing

Ask for one thing at a time: one artifact (or one section of a long one), one substantial question, or up to three small ones. Then end the turn.

Relay every block message you receive verbatim to the engineer, then stop. Every block names the next command.

## Findings

A finding must name the written rule it violates (a criterion, a living-spec statement, a constraint, or a lint rule). A concern with no rule is a preference: do not record it, do not act on it. Never ask or answer "what could be improved" open-endedly. An observation with a concrete consequence goes through `make observe`; hypothesized consequences are dropped.

Dimensions with no entry in `working/standards/budgets.md` are not optimized, by decision.

## Leaving the process

If the engineer says, in any words, that they do not want the process for this work, reply with exactly this and nothing that argues:

    To opt out of the process for this branch:
      1. make opt-out            (optional: make opt-out REASON="<one line>")
         I prepare the record and the commit message. A work item on this branch is archived, not deleted.
      2. make accept STAGE=opt-out
         Run this in your own shell; it signs with your SSH key. I cannot run it for you.
    To rejoin later: make start

## Stop and ask first

A new cloud resource; changes under `libs/shared/`; breaking API changes; persisted data model changes; a new external integration; CI/CD changes; a new runtime dependency; a request that conflicts with a rule here. Flag the conflict, explain the risk, let the engineer decide.

## Where things are

- `working/README.md`: the process, one page; every block message links here.
- `working/spec/`: the living spec, one file per member.
- `working/architecture/constraints.md`: invariants and protected paths, each with its check.
- `working/standards/`: writing standards, criteria templates, budgets.
- `working/history/`: archived work items; read only when asked why something was done.
- `.claude/rules/`: security, testing, conventions, API design, branching, project notes.
- `docs/`: untracked reference; nothing you depend on lives there.

